Yes. If you zoom out far enough, a country starts to look like a scaled-up person with memory, fear, identity, status, resources, alliances, and stories about what other actors intend.

That helps explain why wars happen. States fight when several mechanisms stack together: security dilemmas (“if I don’t arm, I’m vulnerable; if I do arm, you feel threatened”), bargaining failures (both sides think fighting will get them a better outcome than compromise), commitment problems (neither side trusts the other to honor a future deal), misperception, status and identity threats, competition over territory/resources, and domestic incentives where leaders benefit from confrontation even when the population does not.

The narrative layer matters too. Countries maintain stories such as this land is ours, they betrayed us, we are surrounded, weakness invites attack, our dead must not have died for nothing. Once enough institutions and people organize around that story, the narrative becomes materially causal. It changes budgets, alliances, weapons deployments, elections, and eventually battlefield behavior.

AI could make this better or much worse. It can improve translation, prediction, verification, negotiation, early-warning systems, and expose mutually beneficial settlements humans overlook. But it can also accelerate targeting, propaganda, cyberwarfare, autonomous weapons, deception, and decision tempo. The dangerous assumption is that intelligence automatically produces peace. Two highly intelligent agents can still have genuinely incompatible objectives.

So the deeper project isn’t merely “make AI powerful enough to stop wars.” It is to understand the machinery that produces war well enough that AI can help intervene before violence becomes the locally rational move.

A useful abstraction is:

fear / desire → model of other → prediction → incentive → action → other side observes action → updates its model → feedback loop

War is often what happens when that loop becomes self-confirming.

The peace technology, then, is not just pacifism. It is designing mechanisms that let adversaries prove intentions, verify commitments, preserve face, share information, detect deception, and exit escalation without making themselves exploitable.

In other words: before asking AI to end war, we should teach it—and ourselves—why rational actors can recursively manufacture a situation neither originally wanted.